A||N||4||Ozzy||Found the "ticking" noise!!!!||||11||
Z||000000||Ozzy||02-20-2000||05:28 PM||||Hi Gang...<P>Well on it goes. I decided to pull the Valve cover today to investigate what I thought might be a collapsed lifter. (Ticking returned recently)<P>The #1 cylinder intake valve push rod guide has broken allowing the push rod to slide slightly to the left (from rear of vehichle) and also the rocker had turned clockwise about 1/4" to allow the valve end of the rocker to move slightly off the edge of the valve stem!! The push rod is now bent about 1/8". <P>I see no real damage and I can hardly believe it. The rocker was just over the valve stem enough to depress the valve I guess because the lil' beast seems to be running fine(??) <P>I'm just VERY curious what has caused this though. What happened first???  Anyway, for now I'm going to see if I can get a single push rod and rod guide.(??) I'm assuming that you will all tell me I should do all new push rods, lifters etc. I agree but I'm now thinking that this head needs re-built but want to keep the car rolling for now if possible. <P>Nobody seems to be able to clarify if the head off my '84 duke will fit the '88 motor??? If this is possible it's easy for me...I'll just swap 'em out.<P>Thoughts please.<P>P.S.  Having trouble locating the 'push rod guide' thru parts stores. Is this an OEM part??<BR>Ozzy<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 02-20-2000).]||||reg||11
Z||000001||batboy||02-20-2000||06:30 PM||||Ozzy, sounds like you got off lucky compared to what it could of been. Also sounds like you may of caught the problem in the nick of time before you ruined more stuff. If this is a used and abused engine, by all means just fix the minimum. You may have to go to the dealer for that part, if Pontiac don't stock it, try Chevy. Another option is to do a little salvage yard checking. They're bound to have a junk duke head lying around that you can snag a valve guide and a maybe even push rod from. I roll used pushrods on a piece of flat plate glass to see if they're straight. I'd seriously consider replacing that one rocker too, unless you're positive it's perfectly ok. Somebody in the other thread seemed to think the bolt pattern was different on the DIS head. With the valve cover off, you should be able to carefully measure this to see if that's true. Most salvage yards have an interchange book, ask them if they can look it up for you. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||02-21-2000||11:02 AM||||The question I have is: how was the push-rod bent? As far as I know, a push-rod will bend for only the following reasons:<BR>- pistion to valve clearance too tight<BR>- weak push-rod (unlikely)<BR>- stuck valve<BR>- badly worn valve guide.<BR>There may be more but I can't think of any.<P>For the piston to valve clearance thing, since the motor is used, the rod bearings could be bad enought to allow excess piston travel up into the cylindar, thus contacting the valve. Or, a worn or streached timeing belt/chain which allows the open valve to come in contact with the piston.<P>For the stuck valve, does the motor exhibit excess smoke when you start it? I'm thinking possible carbon deposits on the valve guide causing it to stick on cold-starts.<P>If the guide is worn badly, it could push the rocker arm over couse the push-rod to bind in the head (big guess here).<P>At any rate, if it happens again after you replace the bent push-rod you can eliminate the "bad push-rod" theory.<P>Good luck and keep us posted,<BR>Roy L.||||reg||4
Z||000003||Ozzy||02-21-2000||04:51 PM||||Mr fixit...<P>Motor has a fairly recent rebuild on it although done before I bought the car. I suspect the top end was not done. <P>No smoke or sticking valve that I can tell. There is a wear mark on the left side of the push rod guide that broke. It is my thought that the lifter might be shot causing excess 'rattling' of the push rod and causing excessive heat build up on the push rod guide. Because the left side of the guide broke off, allowing the rocker to swivell slightly clockwise, I think the fact that the valve stem side of the rocker arm was sitting over the spring retainer and also that the push rod was not pushing directly vertical all possibly atributed to the slight bend in the push rod.<P>Still trying to locate another push rod guide. Can not find them listed anywhere!<P>Also, this engine has no timing belt/chain. Cam is gear driven.<P><BR>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ozzy||02-22-2000||11:39 PM||||Hi Gang,<P>I finally located some push rod guides at a Chevy dealer today. So...I decided if I'm gonna go to the trouble that I might as well replace all of the lifters, push rods and rockers. The top end was a little bit rattly so maybe this will help with the noise as well.<P>I will know for sure after this weekend.<P>Ozzy||||reg||14
